+		/* --- RA3D layer split -------------------------------------------------
+		 * The NES composes every pixel from a background plane and a sprite
+		 * plane, and GetPixelColor() already evaluates BOTH before choosing one.
+		 * Capturing them separately therefore costs no extra emulation: the
+		 * frontend gets the SAME frame as three images it can place at
+		 * different depths.  Planes, far to near:
+		 *   3 = backdrop (universal colour, opaque, farthest)
+		 *   2 = sprites BEHIND the background (per-sprite priority bit)
+		 *   0 = background — TRANSPARENT where the BG pixel is colour 0
+		 *   1 = sprites IN FRONT of the background
+		 * The background MUST be transparent at colour 0: on hardware a
+		 * behind-priority sprite shows through exactly there, and the backdrop
+		 * shows where nothing else draws.  Filling colour 0 with the backdrop
+		 * colour and calling the plane opaque hides every behind-sprite
+		 * permanently (Punch-Out's opponent against the crowd, Mario's sky).
+		 * Working planes hold palette indices; RA3D_TRANSPARENT means nothing
+		 * was drawn (a real NES palette value is 6 bits plus emphasis, so
+		 * 0xFFFF can never collide).  SendFrame() converts them to ARGB and
+		 * publishes into whichever half of _ra3dPublished the reader is not
+		 * holding, so the frontend never samples a half-written frame. */
+		static constexpr uint16_t RA3D_TRANSPARENT = 0xFFFF;
